{
  "dom_id": "step-q85721",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/f25053c36cf08f57483e619a563c925462699f2b90edf46d6de6fd3034298278.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1$ and $x=y+1$ for $y\\in [0,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/f61bbc9b42b775fbe10077320fe8a31ab905a4b45c8662d211310828fe9cb341.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/c49c2b24b582be974f0e5148fc4898f3b3c39c9b1e6fcb450c496a099f29a4e4.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(y+1)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/17ce7b30d299a6bbef796e11da2589b9c26d760284d3915d472c7fe9103628a5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[y+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}-{y}^{2}+4y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{20}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{20}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+2\\cdot {2}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
